  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 200 PM EDT Friday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Unsettled and humid through Sunday as a frontal \nboundary lingers near the region, bringing scattered to numerous \nshowers, thunderstorms, and localized heavy rain.\n\nA cold front has slowly dropped southward across the forecast area \ntoday, and extends across southern VA and is slightly buckled across \nNE NC. Satellite imagery is highlighting increasing cloud cover \nacross the northern half of the area and partly cloudy to mostly \nclear skies across southern VA and NE NC. There is a relatively \nsharp temperature gradient today due to the aforementioned cloud \ncover. Current highs are in the lower 90 to spotty mid 90s (NE NC) \nacross the south, with northern areas measuring temperatures in the \nupper 70s (mainly Eastern Shore) to the low-mid 80s.\n\nSPC has maintained the Marginal Risk (level 1/5) for areas along and \nsouth of I-64 (excluding the coast) as the boundary slowly moves \nsouthward across the area this evening into tonight. The front will \nbe the focal point of the convection, and local radar is already \ndetecting some development across south-central VA, which are now \nright on the cusp of Lunenburg and Mecklenburg counties. SPC \nmesoanalysis is depicting good instability from generally the Tri-\nCities area and south, which is the best environment for any severe \nis depicted by the Marginal Risk. Shear is not overly impressive, \nbut with steep low level lapse rates, isolated wet microbursts \ncannot be ruled out this afternoon and this evening. The front \ncontinues to sink south tonight, with most of the showers/storms \nending overnight. There continues to be a good signal for low \nstratus and possible some fog across the piedmont overnight. Have \nmaintained mention of fog in these areas, though exactly how far \neast this potential fog creeps is still somewhat uncertain. Humid \nwith lows in the upper 60s to lower 70s south, with somewhat drier \nair to the north where lows drop into the mid 60s. \n\nWidespread precipitation chances peak Saturday afternoon/night, as \nthe stalled boundary lifts back north as a warm front, along with a \nmore pronounced mid-level shortwave disturbances push across the \narea. Forecast soundings show high precipitable water values, modest \ninstability (skinny CAPE profile), and weak steering flow, which \nshould yield efficient warm rainfall processes and additional \nlocalized hydro concerns (supported by an area-wide WPC ERO Marginal \nRisk and a localized upgrade to Slight for portions of the I-95 \ncorridor, including metro RIC. Despite the increasing confidence \nthat localized flooding is possible somewhere in our forecast area, \nthese still remains uncertainty in the exact locations, so have \ndecided to not go with a Flood Watch for Saturday/Saturday night at \nthis time. The HREF, which typically provides a good signal for \nareas where the heaviest rainfall/highest amounts, is not honing in \non any specific area at this time, but will likely start to once we \nget closer to the event on Saturday evening/night. Considerable \ncloud cover will keep a lid on Saturday highs to the low to mid 80s \nN, but highs reach into the upper 80s SE. There may even be some \nweak CAD, potentially keeping far NW zones even cooler (but still \nvery humid). The boundary washes out Sunday, leading to noticeably \nlower convective coverage for the NW 1/2 of the area, but PoPs \nremain high chc to likely for the SE. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Dry with seasonable temperatures early next week, \nwith temperatures warming above normal by midweek as an upper ridge \nbuilds back into the region.\n\nThe upper level flow becomes NW on Monday as a strong upper level \ntrough dives SE from the Great Lakes and pushes off the NE and mid-\nAtlantic coast Monday night, with an upper level ridge slowly \nexpanding NE from the lower MS Valley. This looks strong enough to \ndrive the sfc cold front south of the CWA Monday, and bring \nsignificantly lower dew pts more typical of September. As high \npressure builds in behind the front, we are forecasting mostly sunny \nskies with highs in the 80s Monday. A pleasant night Monday \nnight/Tue AM is on tap, with lows mostly in the low-mid 60s and some \n50s possible across the piedmont and interior MD eastern shore due \nto possible radiational cooling. Diurnal shower and thunderstorm \nchances gradually tick back up mid-to-late week as heat and moisture \nstart to return as the upper level ridge expands E-NE to the TN \nValley and central/southern Appalachians. Temperatures are also \nforecast to climb back to slightly above normal for mid-September, \nwith highs generally in the mid-upper 80s and lows mid-late week. \nOvernight lows will be more reflective of the time of year, dropping \ninto the 60s each night.",
